17592186045417] [17592186537880 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537873 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537880 #edb/ref 1069 "q-90426" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537880 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537880 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x-1}{k})$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x+2}}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.$ What is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537880 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02054798-e30b-5bba-b50a-1ea23b8ac8ca" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537880 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x-1}{k})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5y-1}{k}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5y-1}{k}) \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡((5y-1)/k)} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{5y-1}{k} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ 5y-1 & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k \\\\ 5y & ={3}^{x}\\cdot k+1 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{3}^{x}\\cdot k}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k={3}^{2}=9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537882 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537859 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537882 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250505" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537882 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537882 #edb/ref 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537882 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "04376890-0a44-56a8-b428-8110db81b255" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537882 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2})+1 \\\\ x-1 & =\\frac{1}{4}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ 4x-4 & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2}) & =4x-4 \\\\ {3}^{{log}_{3}⁡(y/4-1/2)} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4}-\\frac{1}{2} & ={3}^{4x-4} \\\\ \\frac{y}{4} & ={3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\ y & =4({3}^{4x-4})+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=4({3}^{4x-4})+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537889 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537763 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537889 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537889 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537889 #edb/ref 1071 "If the function $f(x)=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k?$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+6)/3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537889 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "199584af-715c-57d8-b77d-cfea8258f652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537889 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{3})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3})-k \\\\ 3{log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =x+k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{3}) & =\\frac{x+k}{3} \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/3)} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{3} & ={2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y-1 & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {2}^{(x+k)/3}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=6.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537896 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537810 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537896 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148367" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537896 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537896 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537896 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2340627e-913d-5c02-af0a-4af27f0e0758" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537896 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2x-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{3}-{5}^{2y-k} \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2}{3}-x \\\\ {5}^{2y-k} & =\\frac{2-3x}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2y-k}) & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y-k & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3}) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{5}⁡(\\frac{2-3x}{3})+\\frac{k}{2}$  \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537901 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537759 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537901 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537901 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537901 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+1}-2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537901 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "34bef420-975f-5d0f-9f81-67d4bd446432" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537901 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{x+1}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1}-2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot {e}^{y+1} & =x+2 \\\\ {e}^{y+1} & =2x+4 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x+4)-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537905 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537905 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250507" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537905 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537905 #edb/ref 1071 "If the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k$ has an inverse function given by\n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x-3}+1,\n$$\n\nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537905 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3b5ffb6f-7067-559d-aec0-81e9ef0a06b7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537905 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{x-1}{5})-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5})-k \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(\\frac{y-1}{5}) & =x+k \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡((y-1)/5)} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ \\frac{y-1}{5} & ={2}^{x+k} \\\\ y-1 & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k} \\\\ y & =5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=5\\cdot {2}^{x+k}+1.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537918 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537918 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148278" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537918 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537918 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)={e}^{x+k}+3$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)+5.$ What is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537918 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6181c174-0cfd-5153-a1f3-884abcf7f2a6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537918 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{x+k}+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y+k}+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{y+k}+3 \\\\ {e}^{y+k} & =x-3 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{y+k}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3) \\\\ y+k & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3) \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-3)-k.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-5.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537919 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537919 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81645" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537919 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537919 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-k)$ has an inverse function given by ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}-9}{3}.$ What is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537919 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6267302e-96c5-5885-be5f-0252c2b87de1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537919 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x-k)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k) \\\\ {e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3y-k)} & ={e}^{x} \\\\ 3y-k & ={e}^{x} \\\\ 3y & ={e}^{x}+k \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}+k}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}+k}{3}.$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-9.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537921 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537921 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537921 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537921 #edb/ref 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537921 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "675df8f0-06ab-563c-85f0-2a4a4ed296f3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537921 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{{8}^{6x}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{8}^{6y}}{3} \\\\ 3x & ={8}^{6y} \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & ={log}_{8}⁡({8}^{6y}) \\\\ {log}_{8}⁡(3x) & =6y \\\\ 6y & ={log}_{8}⁡(3x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{8}⁡(3x)}{6}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537940 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537940 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46621" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537940 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537940 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537940 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9b1ff915-f7df-590e-9573-48667059c317" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537940 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(\\frac{y}{3}) \\\\ {10}^{x} & ={10}^{\\operatorname{log}⁡(y/3)} \\\\ {10}^{x} & =\\frac{y}{3} \\\\ y & =3\\cdot {10}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=3\\cdot {10}^{x}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537942 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537942 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250479" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537942 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537942 #edb/ref 1071 "If the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+x/2}+1$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)=2\\operatorname{log}⁡(1-x)-2,\n$$\n\n \nwhat is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537942 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9d666229-2a75-553e-ab1a-3470df672efe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537942 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+x/2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2}+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2}+1 \\\\ \\frac{1}{k}\\cdot {10}^{1+y/2} & =x-1 \\\\ {10}^{1+y/2} & =kx-k \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{1+y/2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k) \\\\ 1+\\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k) \\\\ \\frac{y}{2} & =\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-1 \\\\ y & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=2\\operatorname{log}⁡(kx-k)-2.$\n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=-1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537946 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537818 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537946 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148313" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537946 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537946 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={7}^{x-2}+4.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537946 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2f709a7-c2aa-55fd-a6a5-4424cd8b1fab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537946 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={7}^{x-2}+4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={7}^{y-2}+4 \\\\ x-4 & ={7}^{y-2} \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & ={log}_{7}⁡({7}^{y-2}) \\\\ {log}_{7}⁡(x-4) & =y-2 \\\\ y & ={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{7}⁡(x-4)+2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537960 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537805 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537960 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250404" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537960 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537960 #edb/ref 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)={e}^{2-x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537960 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b31f5c4b-dccc-5d6d-b26c-76fe9889b868" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537960 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={e}^{2-x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{2-y} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2-y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡x & =2-y \\\\ y & =2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537964 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537837 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537964 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46622" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537964 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537964 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537964 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bc418213-398e-54af-8583-a0bcd69d268b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537964 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{2}⁡(x)+8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equations, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{2}⁡(y)+8 \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡(y) & =x-8 \\\\ {2}^{{log}_{2}⁡(y)} & ={2}^{x-8} \\\\ y & ={2}^{x-8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{x-8}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537965 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537853 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537965 #edb/ref 1069 "q-46629" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537965 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537965 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537965 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bf8490ce-55f6-59c1-8771-a458611eb22b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537965 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y) \\\\ {e}^{x} & ={e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7y)} \\\\ {e}^{x} & =7y \\\\ y & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{7}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537976 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537767 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537976 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250405" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537976 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537976 #edb/ref 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)=-{3}^{4x}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537976 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d6cea5e8-bf96-5756-ad7c-875f953f91a2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537976 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-{3}^{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =-{3}^{4y} \\\\ -x & ={3}^{4y} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & ={log}_{3}⁡({3}^{4y}) \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡(-x) & =4y \\\\ 4y & ={log}_{3}⁡(-x) \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(-x)}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537979 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537979 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148359" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537979 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537979 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537979 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e0086fad-533f-5e80-a92f-0ac75b17640c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537979 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2x}-\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2y} & =x+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ {3}^{2y} & =4x+2 \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{2y}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ 2y & ={log}_{3}⁡(4x+2) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{3}⁡(4x+2).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537986 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537986 #edb/ref 1069 "q-250483" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537986 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537986 #edb/ref 1071 "The inverse of the function $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}$ is ${f}^{-1}(x)=${{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537986 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f4092e37-c259-56c3-8698-e5c1a205391f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537986 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:        \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡(y)+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ x-\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{1}{2}{log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ 2x-1 & ={log}_{2}⁡y \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & ={2}^{{log}_{2}⁡y} \\\\ {2}^{2x-1} & =y \\\\ y & ={2}^{2x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)={2}^{2x-1}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537990 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186537792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537990 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148370" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537990 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537990 #edb/ref 1071 "The function $f(x)=\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2$ has an inverse function given by \n\n$$\n{f}^{-1}(x)={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).\n$$\n\nWhat is the value of $k?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537990 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "fcede9b9-3aa7-543c-ba0b-831f64ff8671" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186537990 #edb/ref 1074 "To find ${f}^{-1}(x),$ we start by replacing $f(x)$ with $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{kx}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, interchanging $x$ and $y$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we make $y$ the subject of the equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky}+2 \\\\ \\frac{2}{5}\\cdot {3}^{ky} & =x-2 \\\\ {3}^{ky} & =\\frac{5x-10}{2} \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡({3}^{ky}) & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ ky & ={log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n    \n\nTherefore, the inverse function is ${f}^{-1}(x)=\\frac{1}{k}{log}_{3}⁡(\\frac{5x}{2}-5).$ \n\nComparing our answer with the given expression, we conclude that $k=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
